        "weakestTell": "The sign most often missed: {names} ({counts}).",
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional haulier. First you spend two days working away from the depot, and make five everyday calls on the road: on the train, in the café and its queue, and when a tablet goes missing. You make each call, then you see where it led. After that you sort the safe networks and pages from the look-alikes, a hotel's among them. Only that last part is scored. Nothing you tap is a real network, and no real password is ever typed.",
        "flipHeading": "Later that day",
        "allPhishRight": "Every trap was caught, with every sign named.",
        "noBlameLine": "Reading a screen over someone's shoulder, joining a network named like the café, or leaving a bag for a minute are ordinary slips that happen to careful people; the harm is in the hours before anyone says so, not in the slip. A miss here just shows what to change.",
        "teamLine": "By reporting or checking {n} of these rather than acting on them, you kept a trap away from the rest of your team.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday, three things: turn on the screen lock and auto-lock on your own phone if they're not on already; set your devices to forget public Wi-Fi networks and turn off auto-join, so they don't rejoin a look-alike by themselves; and save the 24/7 service desk number in your phone, so if you lose a work device you can report it on the spot. Report anything at work using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "A look-alike network, or a page after your work password, catches careful people on the road. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gives each one away.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "Public Wi-Fi, in proportion",
            "text": "Nearly everything you do now is encrypted, and Tarrowby's laptops add a VPN (a secure tunnel back to the company) on top, so ordinary work on the venue's own network is mostly fine. The old 'anyone on the café Wi-Fi can read your email' is out of date. What still catches people is a network named to look like the venue's, and a page that asks for your work password."
          },
          {
            "heading": "The look-alike and the over-asking page",
            "text": "You can't tell who runs a hotspot, so ask the venue for the network's name, and don't just pick the strongest signal. A venue's page asks you to accept the terms, sometimes an email or a room number. It never needs your work password; if one asks, close it, use your phone's hotspot, and report it."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Out of sight, out of earshot",
            "text": "In public, someone can see your screen and hear your call. Sit with your back to a wall, use a privacy screen, and lock the screen whenever you look away. On a call, no names, codes or client details; 'I'll call you back' is a complete answer."
          },
          {
            "heading": "With you, or locked away. Lost it? Report it now",
            "text": "A device never sits unattended in a café, a train or a car, even for two minutes. If you lose one, ring the service desk straight away, day or night, even if it's locked: the lock buys time, and the hours before you report are what matter."
          },
          {
            "heading": "What Tarrowby has set up",
            "text": "On Tarrowby's side: the laptops carry a VPN that connects by itself (Tarrowby Connect), every phone, tablet and laptop is encrypted and can be locked or wiped remotely, and the 24/7 service desk can sort a lost device the moment you ring. These are set up for you; your part is to keep the device with you and ring straight away if it goes missing."
          },
          {
            "heading": "At home, mind the camera and what's behind you",
            "text": "Working from home has its own habits. Before a video call, check what's behind you, since whiteboards, wall planners and sticky notes all show up on camera, and share one window rather than your whole screen, so your inbox and other documents stay private. Lock the screen when you step away, even at home, and keep work devices for work, not for family or a quick game."
          }
        ],
